yt-gtk is a GTK3 desktop application that lets users browse YouTube channel feeds, play videos via mpv, view transcripts, and generate AI summaries — all from a local GUI.

The architecture centers on an `AppState` that owns all video data in memory as an `IndexMap<String, Video>`, with a filesystem-backed `Storage` layer that persists a `videos.json` index plus per-video JSON sidecars for transcripts, summaries, and watched state — this split avoids bloating the main index file while keeping metadata queryable. Feed data flows through `feed/fetcher.rs`, which hits the YouTube Data API v3 (playlists, channels, search) and converts RSS items into `Video` structs; all network calls are async (tokio) with exponential backoff and concurrency caps to avoid hammering the API. The UI runs on the GTK main loop but bridges to tokio via a dedicated `Runtime`, communicating state changes through `glib::idle_add` so widgets are only mutated on the main thread. Summaries use a two-tier provider strategy: Gemini Flash is tried first (streaming), then OpenRouter with the fetched transcript as fallback, then raw transcript as a last resort — all three paths emit `StreamingMessage` chunks over an async channel so the UI can progressively render output. Downloaded videos and thumbnails live in well-known subdirectories under the platform data directory, and the `cache/downloader.rs` module spawns external processes (yt-dlp, ffmpeg) under `nice -n 19` to avoid starving the UI process. All YouTube URL construction is centralized in `urls.rs`, which is the single source of truth for watch and thumbnail URL formats.
